To Manjugosha, Lord of Wisdom, ever-devoted prostrations;
To Avalokiteshvara, embodiment of compassion, may you protect every being always;
To Vajrapani, who has unimpeded power and ability, may you conquer every obstacle;
To Arya Tara, who is swift and compassionate, may all activities for the purpose of Dharma be quickly accomplished.

Transference from Dushita;
Entering the mother’s womb; birth,
Skill in artistic disciplines;
Delightedly enjoying a queen and retinue;
Renunciation; performance of austerity;
Approaching the essence of enlightenment;<span class="smw-highlighter pagebreaks" data-type="8" data-state="inline" data-title="Page number from:" title="9" tabindex="0"><span class="smwtticon pagebreak note text-80">[p.9]</span><span class="smwttcontent"><big>[[A Commentary on Shantideva's Engaging in the Conduct of the Bodhisattvas]]</big> <br> <span class="muted">Gyaltsen, Lama Kalsang, and Ani Kunga Chodron, trans. ''A Commentary on Shantideva's Engaging in the Conduct of the Bodhisattvas Written by Sazang Mati Panchen: Known as An Extremely Clear Illumination of the Meaning of the Text''. Walden, NY: Tsechen Kunchab Ling, 2019. First edition published by The Yeshe Dorje Foundation in 2006.</span></span></span>
Conquering the forces of mara; perfect enlightenment;
Turning the wheel of Dharma; and
Passing into parinirvana
Are demonstrated while abiding in the not-completely-purified realms
As long as samsara exists.

As long as clinging to the aggregates exists;
So long will selfclinging exist with them.
Again from selfclinging arises karma;
Againfrom karma arises birth.

It demonstrates that which is not common to others;
Is perfectly complete in relinquishing the defilements of the three realms;
Its own nature is pure. It is purified of stains.
These agree with the four qualities of pure conduct.

That which possesses the meaning of Dharma and is precisely relevant;
Holy words that cause complete abandonment of the defilements of the three realms;
That which demonstrates the benefit of peace;
Such is the holy speech of the Sage. That others is the opposite.<span class="smw-highlighter pagebreaks" data-type="8" data-state="inline" data-title="Page number from:" title="21" tabindex="0"><span class="smwtticon pagebreak note text-80">[p.21]</span><span class="smwttcontent"><big>[[A Commentary on Shantideva's Engaging in the Conduct of the Bodhisattvas]]</big> <br> <span class="muted">Gyaltsen, Lama Kalsang, and Ani Kunga Chodron, trans. ''A Commentary on Shantideva's Engaging in the Conduct of the Bodhisattvas Written by Sazang Mati Panchen: Known as An Extremely Clear Illumination of the Meaning of the Text''. Walden, NY: Tsechen Kunchab Ling, 2019. First edition published by The Yeshe Dorje Foundation in 2006.</span></span></span>

Solely for the purpose of the Buddha’s doctrine;
Explained with an undistracted mind;
Harmonious with and relevant to the path to liberation;
Such should be venerated on one's crown like a sermon of the True Sage.

Meaningless, incorrect meaning, and excellently meaningful;
Hearing, arguing, and exerting in practice;
Essenceless, deceptive, and abandoning suffering;
The shastras are agreed to have the third of each of these three cycles of three.

Rely upon a spiritual teacher who is tamed, peaceful, very peaceful;
Diligent, possesses good qualities, is rich in scripture;
Has thoroughly realized suchness, is skilled in speech;
Is compassionate, and has overcome discouragement.

A lama who possesses good moral conduct, knows the vinaya;
Is compassionate toward the sick, purifies his own retinue;
Strives to benefit through Dharma and material things; and
Bestows timely teachings is to be praised.<span class="smw-highlighter pagebreaks" data-type="8" data-state="inline" data-title="Page number from:" title="23" tabindex="0"><span class="smwtticon pagebreak note text-80">[p.23]</span><span class="smwttcontent"><big>[[A Commentary on Shantideva's Engaging in the Conduct of the Bodhisattvas]]</big> <br> <span class="muted">Gyaltsen, Lama Kalsang, and Ani Kunga Chodron, trans. ''A Commentary on Shantideva's Engaging in the Conduct of the Bodhisattvas Written by Sazang Mati Panchen: Known as An Extremely Clear Illumination of the Meaning of the Text''. Walden, NY: Tsechen Kunchab Ling, 2019. First edition published by The Yeshe Dorje Foundation in 2006.</span></span></span>

A teacher of the exalted Bodhisattva vehicle is gentle;
Free of conceit, undiscouraged;
Clear, uses various types ofreasoning;
Is understandable, not attached to material, and harmonious with all.

By hearing, one will learn Dharma;
By hearing, repel sins;
By hearing, discard meaninglessness;
By hearing, one will attain complete liberation.

Because of having awakened from the sleep of ignorance;
Because of having developed intelligence for all that is knowable;
Buddhas are awakened and developed like a lotus.
Therefore, they are known as Buddhas.

Unattached to desire (giving); three actions completely purified (morality);
Outshining anger (patience); endeavoring in excellent qualities (diligence);
Unwavering in Dharma (meditation); seeing profound suchness (wisdom);
The great enlightened one in Dushita is the Bodhisattva.

Because they rarely appear; because they are immaculate;
Because they possess power; because they are the ornament ofthe world;
Because they are exalted; and because they are unchanging,
They are known as precious jewels.

There is nothing here that has not been explained before,
And I have no skill in composition;
Therefore, I lack the thought this will be of benefit to others.
